🚀 What is ECS?

ECS = Elastic Container Service

ECS is an AWS service used to run and manage Docker containers.

For example, suppose you have a web application:

Your Application
      ↓
Docker Image
      ↓
ECR
      ↓
ECS
      ↓
Docker Container running
      ↓
Users access application

So:

Docker → creates the container/image
ECR → stores the Docker image
ECS → runs and manages the container
1. Main components of ECS

There are several important terms:

ECS
 │
 ├── Cluster
 │
 ├── Task Definition
 │
 ├── Task
 │
 ├── Service
 │
 └── Container

Let's understand each one.

2. ECS Cluster

A cluster is a logical grouping of your ECS resources.

Example:

ECS Cluster
   │
   ├── Task 1
   ├── Task 2
   └── Task 3

Suppose your application is called my-app.

You could create:

Cluster name:
my-app-cluster

Then ECS runs your application inside this cluster.

Important

A cluster itself does not necessarily mean an EC2 server.

ECS has two major ways to run containers:

ECS on Fargate

AWS manages the servers for you.

ECS
 ↓
Fargate
 ↓
Container
ECS on EC2

You manage the EC2 instances.

ECS
 ↓
EC2
 ↓
Docker Container

We'll come back to this.

3. Task Definition

This is very important.

A Task Definition tells ECS how to run your container.

Think of it as a configuration file/instruction manual.

For example:

Task Definition
       |
       ├── Container name
       ├── Docker image
       ├── CPU
       ├── Memory
       ├── Port
       ├── Environment variables
       └── IAM role

Example:

Task Definition: my-app-task

Container:
    Name: my-app

Image:
    123456789.dkr.ecr.ap-south-1.amazonaws.com/my-app:latest

CPU:
    0.5 vCPU

Memory:
    1 GB

Port:
    80

This tells ECS:

"Run this Docker image with this amount of CPU/memory and expose port 80."

4. Task

A Task is a running instance of a Task Definition.

For example, you have:

Task Definition
my-app-task

You tell ECS to run it.

ECS creates:

Task
 └── Container
      └── my-app

If you run 3 tasks:

ECS Cluster
    │
    ├── Task 1 → Container
    ├── Task 2 → Container
    └── Task 3 → Container

So:

Task Definition = instructions

Task = running copy of those instructions

1. Most common: 1 Task → 1 Container

For a simple application:

Task
 └── Container
      └── Cart Application

For example:

Cart Task
   ↓
Cart Container

If your service needs 3 copies:

Cart Service
 ├── Task 1 → Cart Container
 ├── Task 2 → Cart Container
 └── Task 3 → Cart Container
2. One Task → Multiple Containers

You can also put multiple containers in the same Task:

Task
 ├── Container 1 → Main Application
 └── Container 2 → Sidecar

For example:

Task
 ├── Web App Container
 └── Logging Container
These containers are closely related and run together.

Task 1
├── Cart Container 1
└── Cart Container 2


Important distinction
Service
   ↓
Multiple Tasks
   ↓
Each Task can have
1 or more Containers

Example:

Cart Service
   |
   ├── Task 1
   │    ├── Cart Container
   │    └── Logging Container
   │
   └── Task 2
        ├── Cart Container
        └── Logging Container

So Task ≠ Container.

Task is the unit ECS runs. A Task contains one or more containers.

5. Service

This is another very important ECS concept.

An ECS Service keeps the required number of tasks running.

Suppose you want:

Desired count = 3

ECS will try to maintain:

Task 1 → Running
Task 2 → Running
Task 3 → Running

If Task 2 crashes:

Task 1 → Running
Task 2 → ❌
Task 3 → Running

ECS Service notices that only 2 are running.

It starts another:

Task 1 → Running
Task 2 → New Task
Task 3 → Running

Now again:

3 tasks running

That's one of the main purposes of an ECS Service.

6. Container

The actual Docker container runs inside the ECS task.

For example:

ECS Cluster
      ↓
   Service
      ↓
    Tasks
      ↓
 Containers

Example:

my-app-service
      |
      ├── Task 1
      |     └── my-app container
      |
      ├── Task 2
      |     └── my-app container
      |
      └── Task 3
            └── my-app container
7. Where does ECR come into this?

This is where ECR and ECS connect.

Suppose you build your application:

docker build -t my-app .

You get:

my-app Docker Image

You push it to ECR:

ECR
└── my-app
     └── latest

Then your ECS Task Definition says:

Image:
123456789.dkr.ecr.ap-south-1.amazonaws.com/my-app:latest

When ECS starts the task:

ECS Task
   ↓
Pull image
   ↓
ECR
   ↓
Docker Image
   ↓
Container starts
8. Complete ECS architecture

A common architecture looks like this:

                         Internet
                            |
                            ↓
                    Application Load
                        Balancer
                            |
             ┌──────────────┴──────────────┐
             ↓                             ↓
          ECS Task                      ECS Task
             |                             |
        Container                     Container
             |                             |
             └──────────────┬──────────────┘
                            |
                         ECS Service
                            |
                       ECS Cluster

And the images come from:

                     ECR
                      |
                 Docker Image
                      |
                      ↓
                 ECS Service
                      |
                      ↓
                    Tasks
                      |
                      ↓
                 Containers
9. ECS + Fargate

This is probably the easiest way to understand ECS.

With Fargate, you don't manage EC2 servers.

You
 ↓
ECS
 ↓
Fargate
 ↓
Container

You specify:

CPU
Memory
Container image
Port
Networking

AWS manages the underlying infrastructure.

For example:

ECS Cluster
      |
      ↓
ECS Service
      |
      ↓
Fargate
      |
      ├── Task 1
      │    └── Container
      |
      └── Task 2
           └── Container

You don't have to create an EC2 instance yourself.

10. ECS + EC2

Another option is ECS using EC2 instances.

Here you manage the EC2 infrastructure.

ECS Cluster
     |
     ↓
EC2 Instances
     |
     ├── Container
     ├── Container
     └── Container

For example:

ECS Cluster
     |
     ├── EC2 Instance 1
     │      ├── Task 1
     │      └── Task 2
     |
     └── EC2 Instance 2
            ├── Task 3
            └── Task 4
Difference
ECS Fargate	ECS EC2
AWS manages servers	You manage EC2
No EC2 management	Need EC2 management
Easier	More control
Pay for task resources	Pay for EC2 instances
11. ECS Service + Load Balancer

For a web application, normally you don't want users directly accessing individual containers.

You can use an Application Load Balancer (ALB).

Example:

                 Users
                   |
                   ↓
                  ALB
                   |
          ┌────────┴────────┐
          ↓                 ↓
       Task 1             Task 2
       port 80            port 80
          |                 |
       Container         Container

The ALB distributes traffic.

For example:

User 1 → Task 1
User 2 → Task 2
User 3 → Task 1
User 4 → Task 2

If Task 1 becomes unhealthy, the ALB can stop sending traffic to it.

12. ECS networking

ECS tasks need networking.

With the common awsvpc network mode, a task gets an IP address inside your VPC.

For example:

VPC
 |
 ├── Public Subnet
 |
 └── Private Subnet
        |
        ├── ECS Task 1
        │     IP: 10.0.2.10
        |
        └── ECS Task 2
              IP: 10.0.2.11

A common production setup is:

Internet
   ↓
Internet Gateway
   ↓
ALB
   ↓
Private Subnet
   ↓
ECS Tasks

The ECS containers don't necessarily need public IP addresses.

13. Security Groups

You can use security groups to control traffic.

Example:

Internet
   ↓
ALB Security Group
   ↓ port 80/443
ECS Security Group
   ↓
Container

You could configure:

ALB Security Group
Allow:
80
443

from the internet.

ECS Security Group
Allow:
Port 80
Source = ALB Security Group

So users cannot directly access the ECS task.

14. ECS Deployment

Suppose you have version 1:

my-app:v1

You push it to ECR.

ECS runs:

Task 1 → v1
Task 2 → v1

Now you develop version 2:

my-app:v2

Push to ECR:

ECR
├── my-app:v1
└── my-app:v2

Update the ECS Task Definition to use:

my-app:v2

Then ECS Service performs a deployment.

For example:

Old:
Task 1 → v1
Task 2 → v1

Deployment

New:
Task 1 → v2
Task 2 → v2

ECS gradually replaces the old tasks depending on the deployment configuration.

15. ECS with CI/CD

This is where ECS becomes very useful for DevOps.

Typical pipeline:

Developer
    ↓
Git
    ↓
CodePipeline
    ↓
CodeBuild
    ↓
docker build
    ↓
ECR
    ↓
ECS
    ↓
New Task Definition
    ↓
New Tasks
    ↓
Application

For example:

Developer pushes code
        ↓
Git repository
        ↓
CodeBuild
        ↓
Docker image created
        ↓
Image pushed to ECR
        ↓
ECS deployment triggered
        ↓
ECS pulls new image
        ↓
New container starts
