Yes — Task 1, Task 2, and Task 3 can be the same application. In fact, that is the most common use case.

For example, suppose your application is my-app and you want 3 copies running:

ECS Cluster: my-app-cluster
        |
        └── ECS Service: my-app-service
                 |
        ┌────────┼────────┐
        ↓        ↓        ↓
      Task 1   Task 2   Task 3
        |        |        |
      my-app   my-app   my-app
     Container Container Container

All 3 tasks run the same application, usually from the same Docker image:

ECR
└── my-app:v1
      ↓
Task 1 → my-app:v1
Task 2 → my-app:v1
Task 3 → my-app:v1
Why 3 copies?

For example, if 1 container can handle 100 users:

1 Task   → 100 users
3 Tasks  → roughly 300 users

Also, if one task crashes:

Task 1 → ❌
Task 2 → ✅
Task 3 → ✅

The ECS Service can create another task to maintain the desired count of 3.

What if you have another application?

You do not necessarily need a new cluster.

You can put multiple applications in the same cluster:

ECS Cluster
   |
   ├── Service A
   │     ├── Task 1 → App A
   │     └── Task 2 → App A
   |
   ├── Service B
   │     ├── Task 1 → App B
   │     └── Task 2 → App B
   |
   └── Service C
         └── Task 1 → App C

So:

Same application → multiple Tasks

Different applications → usually different Services, but they can share the same Cluster.

You create a new cluster only when you have a reason to separate the applications/infrastructure, not simply because the application is different.








ECS hierarchy
ECS Cluster
     ↓
   Service
     ↓
   Task
     ↓
 Container
What does each one do?

1. Service → manages/runs Tasks

You tell the Service:

"I need 3 Tasks running."

The Service maintains:

Service
  ↓
Task 1 ✅
Task 2 ✅
Task 3 ✅

If Task 2 stops:

Task 1 ✅
Task 2 ❌
Task 3 ✅

The Service starts/replaces Task 2.

2. Task → runs the container

A Task is the running instance of your Task Definition.

Task
 ↓
Container
 ↓
Your application
What about "instance"?

Be careful with this word.

If you're using Fargate:

ECS Service
     ↓
   Task
     ↓
 Container

There is no EC2 instance that you manage.

If you're using ECS on EC2:

ECS Service
     ↓
   Task
     ↓
 Container
     ↓
EC2 Instance

So the best interview answer is:

An ECS Service maintains the desired number of Tasks, and each Task runs one or more containers. With Fargate, AWS manages the underlying compute; with ECS on EC2, the Tasks run on your EC2 instances.






